course: dictionary
lesson: 16163
title: Worse: comparing things
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Worse is the word we use when we compare two things and one is more bad than the other. The pattern is bad, worse, worst: bad is the start, worse compares two things, and worst is the most bad of all.
explain: «My cold is bad today, but it was worse yesterday.» Yesterday the cold was more bad than today, so worse means more bad. To check, swap in more bad: if the sentence still makes sense, worse is the right word.
explain: Worse can also mean more ill, or more badly. «He felt worse in the night» means he felt more ill. «She sang worse than before» means she sang more badly. The words around worse tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Worse is the comparative of bad, and worst is the superlative: bad, worse, worst.
fact: «He felt worse in the night» means he felt more ill.
fact: «To make matters worse» means something bad happened as well, adding to the problem.
fact: In a spelling test a lower score is worse, but in a race a bigger time is worse.
fact: Worse can also be an adverb: «She sang worse than before» means she sang more badly.
exercise: «Ben's cold got worse on Monday.» What does worse mean here? | Finished | Less tired | *More ill | More happy
exercise: Ella scored 3 out of 10 in a spelling test and Max scored 8. Whose result is worse? | Both are the same | Max | Neither | *Ella
exercise: Which word is the comparison of bad? | badly | worst | *worse | bad
test: «The rain was bad at lunch, and by tea it was even worse.» What does even worse mean? | Less bad | The same | *More bad than before | Dry
test: «She sang worse than before.» In this sentence, worse means? | *More badly | Quietly | Very well | Happily
exam: My cold was bad on Monday, but on Tuesday it was ___. | *worse | badly | better | worst
